{ignore}
<p><code>{qr}</code> encodes its first parameter as a QR code and writes it as an inline SVG.
The encoder is PAD's own (<code>lib/qr.php</code>): nothing is fetched and no image is
stored, and the same text is always the same picture, so a page with a code caches and prints
like any other.</p>

<p>The text is encoded in one mode - numeric for digits only, alphanumeric for upper case,
digits and <code> $%*+-./:</code>, else the bytes of its UTF-8 - in the smallest of the 40
versions that holds it at the error correction asked, under the mask with the lowest penalty.
A higher <code>level</code> survives more damage (a logo printed over it, a scratched label)
at the cost of a denser code.</p>

<p>The colours are fixed, <code>#000</code> on <code>#fff</code> unless <code>color</code> and
<code>background</code> say otherwise - not following the page's dark mode, since a reader
needs the contrast and a light quiet zone. The SVG has <code>role="img"</code> and the text
(or <code>title</code>) as its name.</p>

<p>A text too long for version 40 at the level asked, or a level other than L, M, Q or H, is
an error under the strict check, and the tag writes nothing. For a one-dimensional code on a
product or a parcel see <a href="?tag/barcode">{barcode}</a>.</p>
{/ignore}
